name: the gauntlet
brief: multi-command chains, composed from everything the levels taught -- text triage, then the tradecraft you carry a stick like this to do: name a blob by its magic, carve the secret out of it, count who was refused, find the line where a machine died. thirty seconds each, one life, no hints, no reference. every chain is graded on what your pipe really prints, so any correct pipeline passes. how far can you get?
---
q: %count% the distinct lines of @txt@
a: sort -u @txt@ | wc -l
lvl: 05
ref: sort
---
q: %show% only the lines of @txt@ that appear more than once
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -d
lvl: 05
ref: uniq
---
q: %count% the @sev@ lines of @log@ with a pipe, not a flag
a: grep @sev@ @log@ | wc -l
lvl: 06
ref: grep
---
q: %count% the times a connection was refused in @log@, with a pipe
a: grep refused @log@ | wc -l
lvl: 06
ref: grep
---
q: %show% the line where @log@ died
a: grep FATAL @log@ | tail -1
lvl: 06
ref: grep
---
q: %count% the .txt files under this directory, at any depth
a: find . -name '*.txt' | wc -l
lvl: 07
ref: find
---
q: %show% the status column of @csv@ without its header, sorted
a: tail -n +2 @csv@ | cut -d, -f3 | sort
lvl: 08
ref: cut
---
q: %show% the settings in @cfg@ with the spaces squeezed out of the assignments, skipping comments and blanks
a: grep -v '^#' @cfg@ | sed -n 's/ = /=/p'
lvl: 09
ref: sed
---
q: add up the count column of @csv@, skipping the header
a: tail -n +2 @csv@ | awk -F, '{s += $4} END {print s}'
lvl: 10
ref: awk
---
q: %list% every severity that appears in @log@, once each
a: awk '{print $3}' @log@ | sort -u
lvl: 10
ref: awk
---
q: %show% the times of the @sev@ lines in @log@, sorted
a: grep @sev@ @log@ | awk '{print $2}' | sort
lvl: 10
ref: awk
---
q: %get% the address that was refused in @log@, listed once
a: grep refused @log@ | awk '{print $NF}' | sort -u
lvl: 10
ref: awk
---
q: %show% the grand total line count across every .log file under this directory
a: find . -name '*.log' | xargs wc -l | tail -1
lvl: 18
ref: xargs
---
q: %show% just the hash of @txt@, nothing after it
a: sha256sum @txt@ | cut -d' ' -f1
lvl: 20
ref: sha256sum
---
q: {name @bin@ by its first eight bytes in hex, on one line|read @bin@'s first eight bytes as hex, one line|dump the first eight bytes of @bin@ as hex on a single line}
a: od -A n -t x1 -N 8 @bin@ | head -1
lvl: 21
ref: od
---
q: {pull the readable secret buried in @bin@|carve the unlocking string out of @bin@|dig the string that mentions unlocking out of @bin@}
a: strings @bin@ | grep unlock
lvl: 21
ref: strings
---
q: %count% the processor lines in /proc/cpuinfo with a pipe, not a flag
a: grep processor /proc/cpuinfo | wc -l
lvl: 23
ref: grep
---
q: %count% the inet addresses on this machine
a: ip addr | grep -c inet
lvl: 24
ref: ip
---
q: %show% the value of roothash from the kernel command line, splitting it into one word per line first
a: tr ' ' '\n' < /proc/cmdline | grep roothash | cut -d= -f2
lvl: 29
ref: tr
